    "narrative": "For the June 11 slate, Ghost Ride the WHIP leans into high-ceiling profiles facing favorable SP xERAs. The anchor of our lineup is **Corbin Carroll** (77.71), whose elite sprint speed remains our primary weapon; he faces Phillips and benefits from a prime #2 order slot that maximizes his stolen base opportunities despite a slight park penalty. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (76.21) is our strongest pure value play, capitalizing on a significant platoon edge against Rocker and a favorable SP skill profile that elevates his base projection by over 11%. Meanwhile, **Seiya Suzuki** (71.16) and **Josh Naylor** (69.80) round out the core, both thriving on a massive park boost at their respective venue that heavily outweighs the moderate difficulty of facing Bradish.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved the bottom of our starting configuration, as **Dalton Rushing** (51.68) and **Cole Young** (52.70) both hovered near the marginal threshold. **Cole Young** ultimately secured the MI start due to a superior #1 order placement and a massive +16% park factor that offset the risk of Bradish\u2019s 3.68 xERA. While **Zach McKinstry** (44.30) barely cracked the lineup, his inclusion was driven specifically by the \"Slow SP Delivery\" variable, which boosts his efficiency for stolen base potential\u2014a critical factor given our 5x5 scarcity strategy. We continue to monitor the IL status of **Mickey Moniak**, while **James Wood**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, and **Jose Caballero** remain sidelined today due to off-days, forcing us to lean heavily on the remaining active pool of **Austin Riley**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Trevor Story**, **Brice Turang**, **Max Muncy**, **Henry Bolte**, **Kyle Karros**, **Carson Williams**, and **Travis Bazzana** for any necessary late-game substitutions. Proceed with caution on **Gabriel Moreno** (60.46) as his production is contingent on maintaining that #3 spot; any mid-game lineup shuffle would significantly jeopardize his efficiency score.",
